    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2009702172259234291",
      "url": "https://x.com/directormiresi/status/2009702172259234291",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/directormiresi/status/2009702172259234291",
      "text": "WHY GLOBAL WORK HAS A PLOT HOLE CALLED PAYMENTS\n\nDIRECTOR'S PERSPECTIVE by @directormiresi, Film Director & FCO\n\nYou've wrapped production. Your composer in Punjab is waiting. Your bank says \"processing\", again.\n\nHere's what nobody tells you about running a global production: the creative part is easy now. Your motion graphics designer in Brazil can pull an all-nighter and send you polished assets by morning. Your composer in India can score an entire scene without ever stepping on set.\n\nTools work. Talent is everywhere. Collaboration is seamless.\nUntil it's time to pay people.\nSuddenly you're stuck. Wire transfers take 5-7 business days, if they go through at all.\nPlatforms work, sure, but they're expensive and sometimes just block entire regions without warning.\n\nYou try explaining to your composer why his payment is delayed again, knowing he's already moved on to the next project. He asks if you can just pay him in USDC next time. You consider it, but then what? You're juggling wallet addresses, gas fees, trying to figure out which chain he's on, wondering if you copied the address correctly because one wrong character means that money is gone forever, and if you need to pay ten people across five countries? Now you're maintaining spreadsheets just to track who got paid what and where.\n\nThis is where global work breaks down. We've solved communication. We've solved collaboration, but compensation? Still a nightmare.\n\nSTORYTELLER'S PERSPECTIVE by @Prizzy_pretty, Web3 Storyteller & Content Creator\n\nI write threads about blockchain projects, host X Spaces, and craft AI prompts for clients I've never met in person as my work is global by default - devs in Africa, marketers in Asia, designers in America.\n\nDiscord for brainstorming, Notion for outlines, Midjourney for visuals - collaboration is smooth, but payments? That's the daily grind that kills the vibe.\n\nLast month, I hosted a Space for a DeFi protocol. 2,000 listeners, killer engagement, sponsors lined up. My co-host in India crushed the technical breakdown, my co-designer in Philippens dropped fire visuals. We wrapped feeling pumped.\nThen came the payment chaos.\n\nWires from sponsors took 5 days to clear my bank. Fees ate 8% of my cut. Paying my team meant chasing bank details across time zones. One collaborator in Nigeria got his account frozen mid-transfer - no warning.\n\nTwo weeks ago, a Layer-2 project hired me to write a launch thread and design the full visual package. I spent three days in Figma, delivered pixel-perfect assets. The thread went live and hit 200k impressions. Client loved it.\n\nThen came payout time.\n\nThey wired USD. It took 6 days to hit my Nigerian bank, got hit with $45 in fees plus terrible FX rates. I lost almost 10% just to access my own money. Meanwhile, I still had to pay my co-designer who's from the Philippines - another round of delays and charges.\n\nThe creative flow stops dead when compensation lags.\n\nThe final act of every project - the part where people actually get paid for their work is held together by duct tape and frustration.\n\n@allscaleio just hit 10k followers on 𝕏 and is scaling fast for because it's the payment infrastructure global creators have been waiting for.\n\nDual Perspective - a format by MIRESI",
